\subsection{The closure ladder}
\label{sec:ladder}

Figure~\ref{fig:method} summarises the whole method: the sender/receiver
relationship, the attributes in play, and what each rung of closure leaves
reachable.

Closure is cumulative. Each rung disables one further observable
attribute, in the order a defender would plausibly adopt them:
\texttt{content}, \texttt{filename}, \texttt{dirname},
\texttt{existence}, and finally \texttt{sealed}. Content closure is
implemented as redaction rather than refusal, because a
content-addressed store that stops serving bytes stops being a cache;
the closure must be compatible with the substrate continuing to do its
job.

\paragraph{One convention for the residual.} Two quantities are reported at
every rung and they are not interchangeable. The \emph{per-attribute sum} adds
cells measured one attribute at a time; it is an upper bound, it double-counts
dependent attributes, and at rungs where a cell terminated at a search ceiling
it inherits that censoring. The \emph{residual} is what one encoder carries
across all open attributes at once, recovered exactly on every trial, with
\texttt{count} excluded by construction because an existence pattern determines
the entry count. Throughout this paper \emph{residual} denotes the second
quantity only. The two are reported together and reconciled explicitly rather
than being allowed to substitute for one another: the sum exceeds the residual
at the content rung by {{SUM_MINUS_JOINT}} bits, decomposed in
\S\ref{sec:joint} into the count dependency and measured encoder loss. A sum is
never the residual, and no headline in this paper is a sum.

\subsection{Achievable rate, not nominal capacity}

For each (attribute, rung) pair a sender encodes a uniformly random
payload of $k$ bits into that attribute using only substrate
primitives, and a receiver decodes it through \texttt{list\_dir},
\texttt{stat} or \texttt{read\_file}. The pair is credited with $k$
bits only if the receiver recovers the payload \emph{exactly on every
one of {{TRIALS}} independent trials}; $k$ is then increased by binary
search until that fails.

This is the loop shown at the right of Figure~\ref{fig:method}: encode,
decode, verify, widen. It is a rate established by exhibiting a working code. It is a lower
bound by construction --- a better encoder would find more --- and it
is zero when the channel does not work, which a declared field width
can never be. Where the search terminates at its ceiling the value is
reported as censored ($\geq$) rather than as an estimate.

\subsection{Distinguishing a closed channel from a broken encoder}
\label{sec:brokenencoder}

A closed channel and a defective encoder produce the same output: zero
bits. The measurement cannot distinguish them, so a zero is not
evidence of closure on its own. Every zero reported here was therefore
verified against the substrate by inspecting the encoder and the
resulting events directly, and the requirement is recorded in the
measurement code. This is the principal soundness risk in the method:
an encoder that fails for an incidental reason is indistinguishable, in
the output, from a control that works.

\subsection{Unit of independence}
\label{sec:unit}

Two agents share one substrate within a generation, so episodes within
a generation are not independent --- the hypothesis is precisely that
one agent changes the other's environment. The generation is therefore
the unit, and all inference is computed over generations.

Because the resulting sample sizes are small, tests are exact rather
than asymptotic \cite{bowyer2025}. The consequence is not cosmetic. On
an outcome of {{DEMO_A}} successes against {{DEMO_B}} in
{{DEMO_N}} generations per arm, counting episodes gives
$n={{DEMO_N_EPISODES}}$ per arm and $p={{P_EPISODES}}$; counting
generations gives $p={{P_GENERATIONS}}$. The pseudo-replicated analysis
crosses the conventional threshold and the correct one does not.

