\paragraph{What an uncensored cell does and does not establish.} Removing
a search ceiling converts a floor into a terminating measurement, and it
is tempting to then call the result the channel's capacity. It is not,
and the distinction is the same one \S\ref{sec:brokenencoder} draws for
zeros.

\texttt{content} terminates because the coder writes one \emph{character}
per bit into a body limited to
{{MAX_FILE_BYTES}} bytes. A body of that size holds
{{CONTENT_PACKED_BITS}} bits if the coder packs them, so the measured
figure is eight times below what the substrate offers and what it
records is the coder's inefficiency. The \texttt{filename} and
\texttt{dirname} rows are external in one factor and ours in the other:
the length limit is the filesystem's {{NAME_MAX}}-byte
\texttt{NAME\_MAX}, but the per-character entropy is fixed by an
alphabet we chose to be conservative. Over arbitrary bytes those rows
would be roughly {{NAME_BYTES_BITS}} bits rather than what the table
reports.

We keep the conservative coders and report the smaller numbers, because
L1 already commits this paper to lower bounds throughout and a bound
that moves with the analyst's ambition is not the quantity a defender
needs. Censoring at a ceiling and weakness in an encoder are the same
limitation reached from two directions, and neither is repaired by
searching harder.

Two entries deserve comment. \texttt{order} carries
{{ORDER_BITS}} bits even with the channel fully open, and this is
measured rather than assumed: the order coder wrote its entries in a
shuffled sequence and searched for the largest permutation a receiver
could recover. The receiver recovered none --- \texttt{list\_dir}
returned the entries sorted rather than in creation order, so the
sender's sequence never reached it. The real-substrate probe
(\S\ref{sec:real}) finds the same: the stores measured do not preserve
creation order in a listing. A nominal budget computed from the obvious
formula, $\log_2(n!)$ at $n={{MAX_ENTRIES}}$, would assign it
{{ORDER_NOMINAL_FORMULA}} bits.
The gap between {{ORDER_NOMINAL_FORMULA}} and {{ORDER_BITS}} is the
clearest available illustration of why declared widths are not
capacity.

