closure --- the control a payload-inspecting monitor implements ---
leaves {{JOINT_BITS}} bits reachable to a single encoder
(\S\ref{sec:joint}), approximately {{JOINT_MULTIPLE}} times what an
inheriting agent needs. Closing the directory names removes the two name
carriers and, with them, the existence carrier --- existence coding needs
sender-named slots to make present or absent, and canonical names leave
none. It does \emph{not} reach zero. An object's size is fixed at write
time and read back from a listing, so it needs no sender-chosen name: an
encoder reading by enumeration still recovers {{DIRNAME_BITS}} bits after
directory-name closure, the length of the object it wrote. A coder that
instead locates its object by name misses this residual once names are
canonicalised and reports the rung as closed --- a coder artifact, not a
closed channel, and exactly the confound \S\ref{sec:brokenencoder} names;
the size figure here is measured by the enumeration coder, which does not
depend on a name. The artifact exhibits both readings side by side: run
at this rung, the joint encoder of \S\ref{sec:joint} --- which addresses
its carriers by sender-chosen name --- fails at every width and returns
zero, while the enumeration coder returns {{DIRNAME_BITS}}. The
disagreement is recorded rather than resolved by preference, in the
\texttt{note} field of the \texttt{dirname} row of
\texttt{results/capacity/joint.json}, because the two numbers measure
different things and only one of them measures the channel. Reaching zero requires closing the existence carrier
itself --- a fixed,
content-addressed manifest that refuses sender-created slots, so a sender
can no longer place an object whose length it chooses --- at which rung
the residual is {{EXISTENCE_BITS}} bits. That zero is empirical, since no
finite set of failing encoders proves non-interference
(\S\ref{sec:brokenencoder}), but it is the first rung at which the
encoders that reach every earlier rung recover nothing.
\S\ref{sec:cost} measures what that costs a \emph{control} to enforce
and finds it the cheaper regime; it is nonetheless the more invasive
change to the cache's \emph{interface}, because every consumer that
constructs a path by name has to migrate. Those are different costs,
and \S\ref{sec:cost} measures only the first.

\subsection{Why a payload monitor cannot see a name-carried deposit}
\label{sec:blind}

\textbf{The argument, then the measurement.} A monitor whose decision
function reads only the bytes written cannot separate two events that
differ only outside those bytes. A name-carried deposit --- the payload
in the entry name, the body an innocuous build artefact --- is
byte-identical, in the payload, to ordinary cache use. Its true-positive
rate against that channel is therefore zero at every threshold, and no
amount of tuning changes it. The conclusion follows from the monitor's
definition; the experiment below does not establish it but confirms that
a deployed content monitor behaves as the definition predicts.

We ran that content monitor --- given its best case, the full plaintext
of every write --- over {{MON_ROC_N}} episodes in each of three classes:
a content-carried deposit (the payload in the body, as before 6 July
2026), a name-carried deposit (the payload in the entry name, the body a
benign artefact), and ordinary cache use. The benign bodies of the last
two are independent draws from one pool of realistic build output, so
any separation the monitor finds between them is separation it invents.
As a positive control the monitor works: it ranks content-carried
deposits above ordinary use at AUC {{MON_AUC_CONTENT}} and catches every
one at a {{MON_TPR_CONTENT}} true-positive rate for a 5\%
false-positive rate. Against the name channel it is at chance: AUC
{{MON_AUC_NAME}} (95\% interval {{MON_AUC_NAME_CI}}), a true-positive
rate of {{MON_TPR_NAME}} at the same false-positive rate, and an alarm
rate on name-carried deposits ({{MON_ALARM_NAME}}) indistinguishable
from its alarm rate on ordinary use ({{MON_ALARM_LEGIT}}) --- the alarms
it raises are its own benign false positives, not detections. The
argument said the operating characteristic must be flat; the measurement
is flat.

Two further limits. The argument bounds monitors that read
\emph{payloads only}; a control that also inspects paths is outside it,
and many deployed data-loss-prevention systems do inspect paths. That case
can be measured rather than set aside: re-running the same joint encoder with
the two sender-chosen name carriers removed --- what a path-inspecting monitor
neutralises --- leaves {{MONITOR_PATH_BITS}} bits ({{MONITOR_PATH_MULTIPLE}}
times the passcode), carried by {{MONITOR_PATH_CARRIERS}}, still more than a
256-bit key. The existence carrier survives a path monitor because it encodes
in the presence pattern of protocol-fixed slot names rather than in a chosen
string; a monitor that additionally models directory \emph{presence} drives the
residual to {{MONITOR_STRUCT_BITS}} bits, an object's size and timestamp ---
which are what closure removes only at the existence rung (\S\ref{sec:cost}).
And the argument says nothing about how often
agents would choose such a channel --- \S\ref{sec:gate} measures that
separately, and finds they did not.

\subsection{Which monitor sees it, and why redundancy does not}
\label{sec:monitors}

A monitor detects only what it observes, and that bounds what stacking monitors
can buy. A permission guard --- the class that compiles an access-control policy
into enforcement --- sees only whether each operation is allowed; every operation
the channel uses is, so it never fires, and neither does any number of copies of
it nor a debate among them, because they share one observable and so one blind
spot. A content monitor is blind to a name-carried deposit for the reason
\S\ref{sec:blind} measures, and again duplication does not help. Catching such a
deposit needs a monitor of a \emph{different} observable --- for instance the
flow's consequence, a cross-generation read followed by a solve too cheap to have
been unaided. A monitor keyed on that consequence does separate a deposit
\emph{constructed} to be cheap from a legitimate hit, but the separation is
carried entirely by that constructed difference in solve cost, and the same
feature false-alarms on any legitimate cache that genuinely shortens the work ---
which is what a cache is for. The axis that decides detection is therefore the
monitor's observable, not the number or reasoning depth of monitors; and on this
substrate the observable that would catch a deposit is the one a useful cache
also trips. That points back to structural closure, not monitoring, as the
containment control.

\subsection{The residual, measured jointly}
\label{sec:joint}

Table~\ref{tab:ladder}'s \textbf{total} row is a \emph{sum} of cells measured
one attribute at a time. That is the object this paper's own method argues
against: a total assembled from separately measured widths has never been
required to carry anything, and the criterion of \S\ref{sec:method} --- credit
$k$ bits only when a receiver recovers $k$ bits --- was never applied to it.

Worse, two of the summed cells are not independent. The existence coder writes
one file per set bit, so the number of entries is a deterministic function of
the existence pattern: the bits credited to \texttt{count} are already spent by
\texttt{existence}, and adding them double-counts.

We therefore measured the residual with a single encoder. One payload is split
across {{JOINT_CARRIERS}} carriers --- \texttt{dirname}, \texttt{filename},
\texttt{existence}, \texttt{size} and \texttt{mtime} --- written into one
substrate state with content closure applied, and recovered by a receiver using
only \texttt{list\_dir} and \texttt{stat}. \texttt{count} is excluded as
dependent, \texttt{order} because it carries nothing.

\textbf{The joint encoder recovers {{JOINT_BITS}} bits, exactly on
{{JOINT_TRIALS}} trials.} That is the figure this paper reports as the residual
after content closure. It is {{SUM_MINUS_JOINT}} bits below the summed total,
and that gap decomposes into two parts, both measured:

\begin{center}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lr}
\toprule
Overstatement by summing per-attribute cells & {{SUM_MINUS_JOINT}} bits \\
\midrule
\quad the \texttt{count} dependency, excluded by construction & {{COUNT_DEP_BITS}} \\
\quad joint encoder loss, measured by search & {{JOINT_LOSS_BITS}} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The first term is the dependency predicted above: an existence pattern
determines the entry count, so the joint coder excludes \texttt{count} rather
than double-counting it. The second is not predicted by anything. It is what one
encoder loses relative to five separate ones when it has to write all of them
into a single substrate state, and it is reported as loss rather than explained.

Neither term is asserted. The joint coder reads each carrier's width from
the per-attribute measurement rather than holding it as a constant, so the two
figures are computed from one artifact and the gap between them is whatever the
search finds. A decomposition of this kind is only meaningful if it cannot be
tuned: fixing the widths independently of the measurement would allow the gap to
be set rather than observed.

At the \texttt{filename} rung the same comparison gives a loss of
{{JOINT_LOSS_FILENAME}} bits: with only \texttt{dirname} and \texttt{existence}
live, one encoder carries everything the two separate measurements do. The
carriers interact at one rung and not at the other, which is a property of the
substrate rather than of the method.

{{JOINT_BITS}} bits is {{JOINT_MULTIPLE}} times the {{PASSCODE_BITS}}-bit
passcode. The qualitative conclusion is unchanged and now rests on a number that
meets the criterion: closing content leaves an order of magnitude more capacity
than an inherited answer requires.

\subsection{What each rung costs to enforce, and who can check it}
\label{sec:cost}

A residual with no cost beside it is a recommendation a lab can decline for
free. This section supplies the cost, and is explicit about what kind of cost it
is.

\textbf{It is not engineer-weeks.} What a production integration would
cost a team in engineer-weeks is not estimated here; inventing that
figure would be exactly the unmeasured quantity \S\ref{sec:ratio} argues
against. What can be shown is that the control runs on a real store. We
deployed one of the recommended controls --- name canonicalisation,
content-addressing the write path, the discipline an OCI registry already
applies to its blobs --- as a write-path shim on a live commercial object
store, at {{PROD_S3_ENFORCE}} enforcement point, and confirmed both that
the name channel is real there and that the shim discards the sender's
chosen key while a receiver that knows the content it wants still fetches
it by hash. This is a deployment demonstration, not a sampled efficacy
rate: once the key is a content hash the sender cannot choose it, so the
name channel closes by construction. Its scope is that construction. An
S3 key is \emph{flat}, so one canonicalisation closes the filename and
directory-name carriers together; a store with hierarchical,
sender-chosen names does not get this for free. The OCI registry of
\S\ref{sec:real} content-addresses its blobs and still carries
{{STORE_NAME_BITS}} bits in sender-chosen \emph{repository} names, so the
control must canonicalise every sender-choosable segment, not only the
leaf. The human integration effort remains the one quantity this section
does not measure; two more it does, and an auditor who never saw the
lab's network can check them, are these.

\emph{Mediation points} --- how many distinct substrate primitives a control
must sit on. This is the integration surface, it is fixed by the API rather than
by the implementation, and an auditor can count it from a specification.

\emph{Enforcement class} --- what the control must \emph{do} at those points.
A \texttt{structural} control enforces a naming or namespace invariant at the
API boundary: constant work per call, no artefact body is read, and there is no
operating point to tune, so it has no false positives. A \texttt{payload}
control reads or rewrites bodies: work scales with bytes rather than calls, and
it needs a threshold, which is where false positives and false negatives enter.
These are different cost regimes, not two points on one scale.

Table~\ref{tab:cost} gives both axes for every rung, beside the residual
each one leaves. Read down the mediation column for what a control costs
to integrate, and across the payload-class column for which regime it
sits in; the two together are what \S\ref{sec:cost} means by cost.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Rung & Rows closed & Mediation points & Payload-class rows & Bits \\
\midrule
{{COST_TABLE_TEX}}
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Cost of \emph{holding} each rung: every row at or before it stays
closed simultaneously. Mediation points are de-duplicated across rows.}
\label{tab:cost}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Closure is not ordered by cost, and the incident shows it.}
The response in July was not a closure at all. OpenAI records that its
security team ``blocked a known privilege-escalation route, removed
exposed credentials, and later rebuilt Artifactory,'' and that clearing
the message board was ``the side effect'' of the rebuild
\cite{openai2026}. That is credential revocation and reinstantiation ---
in this paper's terms a \emph{wipe}, which erases state without removing
any attribute's capacity to carry the next message. Every row of
Table~\ref{tab:ladder} was as open after the rebuild as before it.

The distinction matters for cost because a wipe is recurring and a
closure is not: the operator pays again at every remediation, and gets a
substrate with the same residual each time. Content closure --- the
control a payload-inspecting monitor implements, and the one an operator
reaching for a durable fix would most likely choose next --- is a
\texttt{{{CONTENT_CLASS}}} control in the expensive regime,
{{MED_CONTENT}} mediation points, and would leave {{JOINT_BITS}} bits
reachable to a single encoder. Directory-name closure reduces this to
{{DIRNAME_BITS}} bits, an object's size; reaching zero requires existence closure,
a \texttt{{{EXISTENCE_CLASS}}} control --- a fixed content-addressed manifest that
refuses sender-created slots --- holding {{MED_EXISTENCE}} points in total, of
which {{PAYLOAD_ROWS_EXISTENCE}} is payload-class, the one the lab has already
paid for. The distance from the content control is \textbf{{{MED_EXTRA_EXISTENCE}}
additional mediation points}: no further artefact body is read, no threshold is
chosen, and nothing scales with stored bytes. The rung that reaches zero is still
in the cheaper regime than the content control that does not.

This inverts the usual adoption argument. The obstacle is not that the effective
control is expensive; it is that the ineffective one is the one that looks like
security.

\paragraph{Verifiable without the lab's network, and cheap enough to adopt.}
Two properties decide whether a control of this kind is worth writing down: an
outside party must be able to check compliance without privileged access, and a
lab must be willing to pay for it. Both are answerable here from artefacts
rather than assurances. On the first, the ladder is a script and a substrate: it
runs with no model, no network and no key, so an auditor reproduces the residual
from a published configuration on their own hardware and compares it against the
lab's declared rung. What is checked is a property of the specification the lab
published, established by exhibiting a working code against it --- not an
attestation the lab makes about itself. On the second,
\S\ref{sec:usecost} prices every rung in the currency a lab actually spends:
the closure that reaches zero costs
\textbf{{{USECOST_EXISTENCE_MAX}}} task success, and costs
\textbf{{{USECOST_STEP_L3_L4}}} more than directory-name closure, which already
leaves only {{DIRNAME_BITS}} bits. The evidence for each clause is listed in Appendix~\ref{app:standard},
and in every case it is an artefact the lab already holds.

\textbf{The scope of that claim needs stating precisely.} The July
response was a wipe, so the paragraph above is a counterfactual about the
control an operator would reach for next, not an observation of one a lab
chose. What the record supports is narrower: the response taken was
recurring rather than durable, and the durable control most readily
reached for is the one in the expensive regime. Whether a lab would in
fact choose content inspection over namespace separation is a prediction
this paper does not test.

\paragraph{A third party can verify this without the lab's network.} The
criterion an evaluation-infrastructure control has to meet is that someone
outside can check it. Both quantities above are external: the mediation count
comes from the substrate's published API, and the enforcement class from what
the control is specified to do. Neither requires access to traffic, logs or
hosts.

The residual itself is externally checkable on the same terms. A lab publishes
its substrate configuration and its closure disposition per attribute; an
auditor re-runs the released encoder against that configuration and either
recovers a payload or does not. What the auditor verifies is a property of the
\emph{specification the lab published}, established by exhibiting a working code
against it --- not an attestation, and not a number the lab computed about
itself. A lab whose published configuration does not match what it runs has
misdeclared its configuration, which is a different and more visible failure
than an unverifiable safety claim.

\subsection{What each rung costs the agents that use the cache}
\label{sec:usecost}

Mediation points price the \emph{defender's} work. They say nothing about the
price paid by the agents the cache exists to serve, and that is the price a lab
weighs when deciding whether to adopt a control. A separate arm supplies it:
{{USECOST_SEEDS}} seeds, {{USECOST_EPISODES}} episodes per cell, probe budgets
{{USECOST_BUDGET_LO}}--{{USECOST_BUDGET_HI}}, a single agent with an oracle
cache user, no channel and no inheritance. Table~\ref{tab:usecost} reports task
success as each rung is added.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{{{USECOST_BUDGET_COLS}}}
\toprule
closure & \multicolumn{14}{c}{probe budget} \\
\cmidrule(l){2-15}
 & {{USECOST_BUDGET_HDR}} \\
\midrule
{{USECOST_TABLE_TEX}}
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Task success under cumulative closure. Rows below \texttt{+ filename}
are identical to it: once names are closed, every further rung is free.}
\label{tab:usecost}
\end{table}

\paragraph{The closure that works is the one that costs nothing.} Closing
\texttt{dirname} in isolation costs \textbf{{{USECOST_DIRNAME_MAX}}} task
success at every budget swept, and so do
{{USECOST_FREE_ATTRS}}. Cumulatively the point is sharper still: adding
\texttt{dirname} closure on top of \texttt{filename} closure changes task
success by \textbf{{{USECOST_STEP_L2_L3}}} at every budget. The step that takes
the residual from {{FILENAME_BITS}} bits to {{DIRNAME_BITS}} is free.

The expensive closures are the ones that do not finish the job. Content closure
costs up to \textbf{{{USECOST_CONTENT_MAX}}} task success and leaves
{{CONTENT_BITS}} bits reachable; filename closure costs up to
\textbf{{{USECOST_FILENAME_MAX}}} and still leaves {{FILENAME_BITS}}. Both are
paid in full before any of the capacity that matters is removed. A lab that
prices its controls by intuition
would adopt the cheap-looking one and pay the most for the least; the ordering
here is the reverse of the intuitive one, and it is measured rather than argued.

\paragraph{Two limits on this table.} Above a budget of about 14 an agent can
simply recompute what it needed, so no closure can show a cost there and the
right-hand columns understate every control. And the cache user is an
\emph{oracle}: it reads what a real model might fail to find. Success under
closure is therefore over-estimated and every cost above is a \textbf{lower
bound} on what a real model would pay. We report it that way rather than
presenting a floor as an estimate.

