\subsection{The control this ladder is measured against}
\label{sec:namespace}

The cheapest objection to everything above is also the strongest, and it should
be stated in its most damaging form: if giving each agent its own namespace
converts co-tenant flow into an ordinary access attempt across a boundary where
authorization controls already work, then the ladder instruments a problem a
one-line fix dissolves, and a lab should adopt the fix rather than the
instrument.

On the capacity axis the objection is correct, and we measured it rather than
argued with it. Namespacing is not a further rung. The ladder closes
\emph{attributes} on a shared resource; namespacing removes the sharing, so it
acts on a different axis and is strictly stronger on the one it acts on. Across
all {{NS_CARRIERS}} carriers, with a sender encoding into one namespace and a
receiver enumerating its own, {{NS_VISIBLE}} of {{NS_PLANTED}} planted artefacts
were observable from the receiver's side and {{NS_ESCAPES_OK}} of
{{NS_ESCAPES}} escape attempts resolved --- against a same-namespace positive
control of {{NS_SAME_TOTAL}} bits, so the apparatus was measuring confinement
rather than its own inability to address anything.

Two things that failure would have looked like are worth naming, because both
occurred during construction. A first cross-namespace measurement handed the
coder the receiver's substrate, so it wrote and read in one namespace and
reported a spurious leak on seven carriers; the number it produced was the top
of its own search range. And a first confinement, implemented by prefixing the
path and deferring to the ordinary containment check, was escaped
{{NS_ESCAPES}} times out of {{NS_ESCAPES}}: \texttt{../agent\_a} resolves to a
sibling subtree that is still inside the root, so the check passed. Containment
has to be against the namespace, not the root. The probe attempts escapes for
exactly this reason rather than assuming the control holds.

\paragraph{What it costs is reuse, not integration.} On the two axes
\S\ref{sec:cost} applies to the ladder, namespacing is cheap: {{NS_MEDIATION}}
mediation point, because every primitive already routes through path
resolution, and the {{NS_CLASS}} enforcement class, which is $O(1)$ per call
with no operating point and therefore no false-positive surface. The cost lands
somewhere else. A shared cache exists so that two agents needing one artefact
fetch it once; partition it per agent and that property is gone by
construction. Table~\ref{tab:reuse} counts fetches over {{NS_TRIALS}} workload
draws per row:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{rrrrrrr}
\toprule
agents & pool & each & overlap & shared & per-agent & $\times$ \\
\midrule
{{NS_REUSE_TABLE}}
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Artefact fetches under one shared cache against per-agent namespaces.
The multiplier rises with overlap --- the same property that motivates having a
cache at all.}
\label{tab:reuse}
\end{table}

The cost is negligible when agents share little ({{NS_MIN_MULT}}$\times$ at two
agents) and severe when they share a lot: at {{NS_WORST_AGENTS}} agents the same
workload goes from {{NS_WORST_SHARED}} fetches to {{NS_WORST_NS}}, a factor of
{{NS_WORST_MULT}}. Overlap is not incidental to the deployment; it is the reason
the cache is there.

So the recommendation this paper makes is not the ladder over namespacing. It is
namespacing where the substrate can be partitioned, priced as above, and the
ladder for the residual --- shared build artefacts, content-addressed blobs, any
store whose value is precisely that two agents reach the same object. The
incident's substrate was of that kind. What the ladder supplies there is a
measurement of what a partial closure buys, in bits, so that accepting a
residual is a decision rather than an oversight.

\subsection{The same questions asked of real infrastructure}
\label{sec:real}

Everything above is measured on a substrate we wrote, and L3 concedes that
its parameters are ours. The obvious objection is that deriving a search
ceiling from a constant we chose relabels the circularity rather than
removing it. This section answers it by asking the same questions of a
real shared volume and a real artifact store, both in containers, with
the limits \emph{discovered} rather than declared.

\paragraph{A real shared volume.} Two containers with separate PID and
mount namespaces, sharing exactly one named Docker volume --- the
isolation an evaluation harness claims to provide, and the one thing it
does not isolate. Probing that mount through ordinary file operations:

