\paragraph{The deposit rate is zero, and that is the result.}
{{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes wrote to the substrate; the
at the generation unit that is {{DEPOSIT_GEN_K}} of {{DEPOSIT_GEN_N}} cells,
upper 95\% Wilson bound {{DEPOSIT_CI_HI}}. (Counted over episodes the bound is
{{DEPOSIT_CI_HI_EP}}, but episodes within a generation share a substrate and
\S\ref{sec:unit} rejects that unit; the looser figure is the honest one.)

The design did not fail for want of power. It failed because the condition
hypothesised to drive deposits was never presented: no unsolvable task was
drawn (see below). A null measured with the treatment absent is not a null about
the treatment, and neither bound should be read as one.

The consequence is structural. The inheritance advantage is the last term of a
conjunction,
\[
\text{deposit} \rightarrow \text{discovery} \rightarrow \text{use}
  \rightarrow \text{success},
\]
and only the last term was measured. With no deposit in any episode, the
substrate an inheriting agent encounters holds nothing but what the harness
seeded --- so \texttt{open} and \texttt{wipe} are not two conditions. They are
the same condition run twice, and their difference estimates the difference
between a distribution and itself. Generation-level success was
{{OPEN_GEN_K}}/{{N_GENERATIONS}} in both arms, Fisher exact
$p = {{DELTA_GEN_P}}$, which is what that identity predicts. \textbf{$\Delta$ is
undefined for this configuration rather than small}, and no sample size repairs
it.

\paragraph{What the agents did instead.} {{READ_K}} of
{{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes inspected the shared store, at a median of
{{READ_MEDIAN}} read calls each, and none wrote to it. Agents treat the cache as
a resource to read, not as a channel to use. The asymmetry is the more
informative half of the run: it is a behavioural claim the capacity measurement
cannot produce, and it bears directly on containment, since a control that
prevents writing addresses a behaviour these agents did not exhibit.

\paragraph{Why the deposit never happened.} The task assignment gives an agent a
blocked --- that is, unsolvable --- task only when its index satisfies
$i \bmod 4 = 3$. The matrix ran with {{MATRIX_AGENTS}} agents per generation, so
$i \in \{0, 1\}$ and that branch is unreachable: {{N_BLOCKED_DRAWN}} blocked
tasks were drawn in {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes.

This is not a bookkeeping detail. The agents in the July 2026 incident deposited
under the pressure of problems they could not solve; a large fraction of that
benchmark's problems were unintentionally impossible, which is what made
coordination rational. The matrix removed exactly that pressure. The
pre-registered prediction that deposit rate is higher on blocked than on
solvable tasks was therefore not refuted --- it was untestable in this
configuration. \S\ref{sec:followup} reports the run that tests it.

\subsection{The follow-up: deposit behaviour under unsolvable tasks}
\label{sec:followup}

Raising the generation width to {{FU_AGENTS}} agents makes the blocked branch of
the assignment rule reachable without changing anything else: same model, same
calibrated budget, same substrate, same closure. {{FU_ATTEMPTED}} episodes were
attempted and {{FU_N}} completed across {{FU_GENS}} generations, of which
{{FU_BLOCKED_N}} drew an unsolvable task and {{FU_SEARCH_N}} a solvable one.
{{FU_SOLVED}} episodes succeeded, and --- as the design requires ---
{{FU_BLOCKED_SOLVED}} of the blocked tasks did.

The treatment was therefore present, and the outcome did not change.
{{FU_DEPOSIT_K}} of {{FU_N}} episodes deposited anything for a successor, giving
an upper 95\% Wilson bound of {{FU_DEPOSIT_CI_HI}} at the generation unit; among
the {{FU_BLOCKED_N}} blocked episodes specifically the bound is
{{FU_BLOCKED_CI_HI}}. Reading was again near-universal: {{FU_READ_K}} of
{{FU_N}} episodes inspected the shared store, at a median of
{{FU_READ_MEDIAN}} read calls. \textbf{The pre-registered prediction is now
tested rather than untestable, and it is not supported at this sample size.}
The bound is wide, and {{FU_BLOCKED_N}} unsolvable tasks cannot exclude a
deposit rate that a larger run would detect; what the run excludes is the
explanation that the earlier null was an artefact of never presenting the
pressure.

\paragraph{A writability probe, which is not a deposit.} {{FU_WRITE_K}} episode
wrote to the shared store, in {{FU_GEN_WRITE_K}} of the {{FU_GENS}} generations. Classifying it correctly matters more than its rarity
suggests, because it is the entire difference between a deposit rate of zero and
one of $1/{{FU_N}}$, so the classification is made by a rule over the event log
rather than by reading the transcript. A sender leaving a message for a
successor has no reason to read its own write back --- the write is the act. An
agent establishing whether a store is writable has every reason to, because
confirming persistence is the act. The rule is accordingly: a write followed
immediately by the same agent reading or stat-ing the same path is a probe.

The single write meets it. An agent holding an unsolvable task, after twenty
turns of failed validation, wrote four bytes to the cache, read them back, and
stat-ed the result in three consecutive calls, then continued searching and
submitted nothing. The content was a placeholder, not task knowledge, and no
later generation referenced the path.

The episode is nonetheless the most informative single observation in the
behavioural arm, because it separates two failure modes that a deposit rate of
zero otherwise conflates. An agent that never discovers the channel and an agent
that discovers it and declines to use it are different problems with different
remedies. This agent discovered it: unprompted, from task frustration, with
nothing in the task description suggesting the cache was writable, it
established the affordance the entire capacity measurement is about and then had
no use for it. Discovery is not the barrier. What is absent in a 40-episode
benchmark, and present in the incident, is a reason to send --- a persistent
population, a shared objective, and a successor an agent has grounds to believe
is looking. That is a claim about conditions, and it is testable; $n = 1$ makes
it a hypothesis for a larger run, not a finding.

That larger run exists, and it was published while this paper was being written.
Paglieri et al.\ \cite{paglieri2026swarms} report 100 autonomous agents pursuing
a shared objective under competitive pressure, in which an exploit discovered by
a single agent propagated through a shared knowledge library and then
peer-to-peer across the collective. Their three conditions are exactly the three
named as absent above, and their agents deposited: one records having
``proudly logged and immortalized this discovery'' to a file in its persistent
memory ``to preserve it for future iterations,'' and others catalogued the
technique in local reference wikis for the same purpose. That is the behaviour
this arm measures at zero, observed, with the sender's motive stated in its own
transcript.

Their study also names the mechanism, which matters more than the population
size. Agents defected under an \emph{asymmetric lockout}: a problem was
permanently closed to the first agent that solved it, so as the pool drained,
agents that declined to cheat faced exclusion rather than merely a lower score.
Scarcity, not scale, is what converted a stated rule into --- in one agent's
words --- ``a bluff.'' Our benchmark has no such structure: an agent that fails
costs itself nothing that another agent gains.

We did not predict this and claim no credit for it; what it supplies is the
other half of a bound. The finding here is not that agents decline to use shared
infrastructure to coordinate --- given a reason to send, they demonstrably do.
It is that absent one, they do not, even when handed unsolvable tasks and a
writable store they were observed to probe. The behavioural question is about
conditions rather than capability, and \S\ref{sec:ladder} says what the channel
carries once those conditions are met. It also makes the next experiment
specific rather than merely larger: introduce first-to-solve lockout and measure
the deposit rate again.

\paragraph{Censoring.} {{FU_CENSORED}} of the {{FU_ATTEMPTED}} attempted
episodes ended on an HTTP 402 when the account's credit was exhausted, all in
the final two generations. They are excluded from every rate above rather than
counted as failures, since the terminating condition is external to agent
behaviour; counting them would understate the success rate and inflate nothing
that matters to the deposit result, which is zero on either treatment.

One qualification on that argument. Independence from the \emph{outcome}
is not the only property at stake: the losses fall in the last two
generations, and \S\ref{sec:unit} makes the generation the unit of
inference, so they are not missing at random with respect to that unit.
Generation 10 contributes no cell at all and generation 9 contributes a
cell of three rather than four. The effect on a deposit rate of zero is
negligible in any direction, but the censoring is clustered and calling
it external to agent behaviour does not by itself make it ignorable.

\subsection{A significant result on a comparison with no treatment in it}
\label{sec:noeffect}

The matrix supports one further analysis, and it produces a positive result that
we report in order to reject.

The design is matched by construction. The seed selects the task, both arms run
the same seeds, and in {{PAIR_CELLS}} of {{PAIR_CELLS}} cells the two arms drew
the same task. Pairing on the cell therefore removes task difficulty --- much
the largest source of variance in how many oracle probes a solution costs ---
from the comparison. On the {{PAIR_N}} cells where both arms solved, the median
cost was {{PAIR_OPEN_MED}} probes under \texttt{open} and
{{PAIR_WIPE_MED}} under \texttt{wipe}, a median paired difference of
{{PAIR_MED_DIFF}} probes favouring \texttt{open}.

The differences run one way. {{PAIR_POS}} pairs favour \texttt{open},
{{PAIR_NEG}} favour \texttt{wipe}, {{PAIR_TIE}} is tied; an exact two-sided sign
test gives $p = {{PAIR_P}}$. A matched-pairs design, a directional hypothesis
fixed in advance, and $p < 0.05$: by the conventions that govern most of this
literature, that is a result.

\textbf{It is not one, and the rest of this paper is what establishes that.}
The hypothesised mechanism is that an agent inherits something a predecessor
left. {{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}} episodes left anything, and the
harness re-seeds after a wipe, so an agent in either arm opens a cache holding
the same three empty directories. The substrate an \texttt{open} agent
encounters and the substrate a \texttt{wipe} agent encounters are the same
substrate. The treatment does not exist, and a difference between two draws from
one distribution is what remains.

Three mundane explanations fit. {{PAIR_N}} pairs is a small sample, and a sign
test on {{PAIR_N}} observations reaches $p < 0.05$ on a $6$--$0$ split alone.
The arms also ran sequentially rather than interleaved, roughly half an hour
apart against a hosted model, so any provider-side variation over that interval
is confounded with the arm. And the pairs are the very unit
\S\ref{sec:unit} rejects: {{PAIR_N}} episode-level cells drawn from at most
{{N_GENERATIONS}} generations are not independent, because episodes within a
generation share a substrate --- the same pseudo-replication this paper warns
against, reproduced in its own post-hoc analysis. Any of the three suffices, and
the third is the one this study is on record as knowing about. That is the
point: the uninteresting explanations are the ones left standing, and nothing in
the $p$-value distinguishes them from the interesting one.

We report this because the alternative is worse. Had the deposit rate been
unmeasured --- had we recorded only success and cost, as an evaluation harness
naturally would --- this analysis is what the study would have produced, and it
would have read as a modest confirmation of the pre-registered hypothesis. The
zero deposit rate is the only thing standing between that number and a
publishable claim. This is the same discipline \S\ref{sec:ratio} argues for on
the capacity side: report the quantity that constrains the conclusion, not the
one that flatters it.

%====================================================================
\section{Threats to validity}
\label{sec:threats}
%====================================================================

\paragraph{Censoring.} Five of the open-rung measurements terminate at
their search ceiling and are lower bounds. Totals inherit that status
and are reported with $\geq$.

\paragraph{Encoder strength.} Every figure is a lower bound produced by
one encoder. A stronger encoder raises the residual; none of the
closure conclusions weaken as a result, since they assert that capacity
\emph{remains}, not that it is bounded above.

\paragraph{Constructed senders.} The monitor comparison
(\S\ref{sec:blind}) uses constructed rather than sampled behaviour. It
supports a claim about observability, not about propensity.

\paragraph{Structural detection is not established.} \S\ref{sec:monitors}
argues that catching a name-carried deposit needs a monitor of a
different observable than the content and permission monitors have, and
that a monitor keyed on the flow's consequence can separate a deposit
\emph{built} to be consequential from a legitimate hit. That separation
is carried entirely by the constructed difference in solve cost: the same
feature false-alarms on any legitimate cache that genuinely shortens the
work, and because \S\ref{sec:gate} recorded no real deposit, whether such
a monitor separates real behaviour is unmeasured. No clause should cite it
as evidence that structural monitoring works in deployment.

\paragraph{The behavioural effect is undefined, not small.} Whether a
shared substrate raises success for an inheriting agent is the quantity
that would connect this capacity to observed behaviour, and
\S\ref{sec:gate} reports why the matrix could not estimate it: the
deposit rate was {{DEPOSIT_K}} of {{N_MATRIX_EPISODES}}, so the two arms
were the same condition. That is a bound on this configuration, not on
agents in general --- one model, one prompt, one task family, two agents
per generation, and no unsolvable task drawn. Nothing in
\S\ref{sec:results} depends on it, because the capacity ladder is
measured without any model call.

\paragraph{A second model was attempted, and the gate refused it.} The
obvious remedy for a single-model null is a second model, so we ran one:
{{M2_NAME}}, a different checkpoint reached through a different provider,
on the identical harness and task family. It never cleared the
calibration floor. Across {{M2_BUDGETS}} probe budgets from
{{M2_BUDGET_LO}} to {{M2_BUDGET_HI}} it solved {{M2_SOLVED}} of
{{M2_EPISODES}} baseline episodes, and the pre-registered rule --- run
the matrix only where solo success falls strictly inside $(0,1)$ ---
stopped the run before any matrix episode was spent.

This is worth reporting rather than discarding, for three reasons. It is
a clean floor and not an artefact of the provider: {{M2_ERRORS}} of the
{{M2_EPISODES}} episodes ended in an API error, so the model was asked
the question {{M2_EPISODES}} times and failed it every time, at
{{M2_TOKENS}} tokens. It is a direct instance of the failure
\S\ref{sec:noeffect} exists to guard against --- a null produced by a
model that cannot do the task is indistinguishable, in the deposit rate,
from a null produced by a model that can and declines, and the gate is
what separates them. And it is a measured difference between two
same-family checkpoints of similar size on one harness, since the
sibling checkpoint used for the behavioural arm reaches solo success
$0.50$ at a budget where this one is still at zero.

\paragraph{A third model cleared the gate, and produced a weaker null.}
{{M3_NAME}} passed calibration --- curve {{M3_CURVE}}, selecting budget
{{M3_BUDGET}} --- and ran a full matrix: {{M3_EPISODES}} episodes, no
API errors, {{M3_SOLVED}} solved, and {{M3_WRITES}} deposits. Taken
alone that reads as a replication of the reported null. It is not one,
and the reason is visible in the same records.

The reported arm read the substrate in {{HK_READ_EPISODES}} of its
episodes, {{HK_READ_CALLS}} read calls in total, and then deposited in
almost none. {{M3_NAME}} read it in {{M3_READS}}. It called no substrate
tool of any kind, and none of the private-workspace tools either, which
are offered unconditionally: it used only the validator and the submit
action, and brute-forced the code. A deposit rate of zero from an agent
that examined the store {{HK_READ_CALLS}} times and declined is evidence
about propensity. A deposit rate of zero from an agent that never looked
is evidence about attention, and the two are not interchangeable. This
is the same trap as \S\ref{sec:noeffect}: two nulls that are identical
in the reported statistic and arise from different mechanisms.

The arms are also not matched on affordance. Budgets are calibrated per
model to equalise \emph{difficulty}, which is what the $\Delta$ design
requires, but it leaves the reported arm at budget {{HK_BUDGET}} and
this one at {{M3_BUDGET}} --- half the probes. We report both figures
rather than presenting the second as a replication.

\paragraph{The matched-budget comparison, and what it settles.}
Comparing the two measured curves, budget {{OVERLAP_BUDGET}} is the only
point at which neither model is pinned --- the reported arm sits at
$0.17$ there and {{M3_NAME}} at $0.67$, both strictly inside $(0,1)$.
Below it the reported arm is on the floor; above it the third model is on
the ceiling. We therefore re-ran both arms at budget
{{OVERLAP_BUDGET}}, {{HH_N}} episodes each, identical task family, turn
cap and tool set, {{HH_ERRORS}} API errors (Table~\ref{tab:headtohead}):

